// Generated by scripts/build-knowledge.mjs from ../references. Do not edit.
export const KNOWLEDGE = "<reference file=\"scoring_criteria.md\">\n# Scoring Criteria\n\nEach dimension scores 0–100. Total = Σ (dimension score × weight). Order: compliance → attributes → selling-point coverage → clarity and order → persona and pain points. Fetch with marketplace parameters (`multi_site_adaptation.md`) and score against that marketplace's rules.\n\n## Weights\n\n| Dimension | Weight |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Compliance | 25% |\n| 2. Attribute completeness | 20% |\n| 3. Selling-point coverage | 20% |\n| 4. Bullet clarity and order | 20% |\n| 5. Buyer persona and pain points | 15% |\n\nGrades: 90–100 Excellent · 75–89 Good · 65–74 Fair · below 65 Poor.\n\n---\n\n## Dimension 1: Compliance (25%)\n\n| Item (JSON key) | Max | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Title length (`title_length`) | 25 | Within limit (US 75; India 75; others per Seller Central) = 25; over by 1–25 = 10; far over = 0 (**P0**) |\n| Title content (`title_content`) | 20 | No banned characters, promo/subjective words, false claims, competitor brands = 20; −5 to −8 per issue (all-caps brand −2, filler like \"original\" −3); several = 0 (**P0**) |\n| Main image (`main_image`) | 20 | White background + no added text + ≥85% fill + ≥1000 px = 20; −5 per issue; added text/watermark = 0 (**P0**) |\n| Bullet content (`bullet_content`) | 15 | No price, promotion, shipping, contact, review quotes = 15; −5 each (**P0**) |\n| Variations (`variation`) | 10 | Same category + consistent naming = 10; inconsistent naming −2 to −5; mixed categories = 0 (**P0**) |\n| Images/video (`image_video`) | 10 | No watermark, unauthorised logos, misleading or health claims, AI people without XMP = 10; −3 each |\n\nChecklist: title length (record the number); characters; promo words; unsupported claims; competitor brands; main image white / no text / fill / resolution; bullets; variations and MRP per variant; secondary images; XMP; suppression.\n\nSummary line: \"Title X/limit characters, [compliant / N issues]; main image [ok/not]; bullets [ok/N issues]; variations [ok/not].\"\n\n---\n\n## Dimension 2: Attribute completeness (20%)\n\n| Item | Max | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Structured fields (`structured_fields`) | 35 | Key-attribute fill ≥90% = 35; 70–89% = 25; 50–69% = 15; <50% = 0 (**P0**) |\n| Key specifications (`spec_completeness`) | 25 | Complete = 25; −5 per missing or wrong key spec |\n| Variation attributes (`variation_attrs`) | 15 | All mapped = 15; partial −5 to −10 |\n| Item Highlights (`highlights_filled`) | 10 | Filled = 10; empty = 0 |\n| A+ / description (`aplus_exists`) | 10 | A+ = 10; text only = 5; none = 0; A+ for the wrong variant = 5 |\n| Search terms (`search_terms`) | 5 | Well used = 5; unknown = 3; brand names or waste = 0–2 |\n\nKey attributes by category:\n\n| Category | Attributes |\n|---|---|\n| Food / grocery | Ingredients with %, diet type, allergens, storage, shelf life, nutrition per 100 g/ml, net quantity, container, FSSAI licence (India) |\n| Furniture / home | Dimensions, material, colour, load, assembly, weight |\n| Electronics | Power/voltage, ports, compatibility, certifications |\n| Kitchen | Capacity, material (food grade), dimensions, dishwasher safe, heat range |\n| Apparel | Size chart, fabric, care, weight |\n| Toys / baby | Age, certifications, material safety |\n| Outdoor | Dimensions, load, waterproof rating, material |\n\nIf backend fields can't be seen, score on visible data, say so, and rescore when the seller shares Seller Central screenshots. An empty \"Product overview\" table doesn't prove attributes are empty.\n\n---\n\n## Dimension 3: Selling-point coverage (20%)\n\n| Item | Max | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Size / quantity (`size_spec`) | 15 | Exact = 15; vague = 5; missing = 0 |\n| Material / composition (`material`) | 15 | Specific (incl. % for food) = 15; generic = 5; missing = 0 |\n| Core function with numbers (`core_function`) | 20 | Quantified = 20; −4 per feature without numbers; adjectives only = 0 |\n| Use case / buyer (`scene`) | 15 | Clear = 15; vague = 5; missing = 0 |\n| Assembly / preparation / how to use (`assembly`) | 15 | Clear = 15; partial = 8; missing = 0 |\n| Differentiation (`differentiation`) | 20 | Real reason to choose this = 20; generic = 10; none = 0 |\n\nTrust facts (what's in the box, manuals, test data) can add value but aren't scored. **Warranty is never required; don't deduct for its absence.** Warranty promises in bullets are a compliance risk.\n\nCoverage matrix: item → which bullet → quality → gap.\n\n---\n\n## Dimension 4: Bullet clarity and order (20%)\n\n| Item | Max | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| One topic per bullet (`single_topic`) | 20 | −5 per mixed bullet; 3+ mixed = −15 |\n| No repetition (`dedup`) | 15 | −4 per repeat; many = −10 |\n| Order (`order`) | 25 | Core benefit first, support in the middle, trust/package last = 25; core benefit not first −10; packaging or background first −15 |\n| Language (`language`) | 20 | Natural local language = 20; literal translation −8; wrong language = 5 |\n| Readability (`readability`) | 10 | −2 to −3 per issue (long sentences, no headers, jargon) |\n| Consistency (`consistency`) | 10 | −4 per conflict between title, bullets, images, attributes (serious conflicts are also compliance P0) |\n\nPer-bullet table: # → text → topic → clarity → issue → rewrite.\n\n---\n\n## Dimension 5: Buyer persona and pain points (15%)\n\n| Item | Max | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Persona (`persona_quality`) | 20 | Specific = 20; vague = 10; none = 0 |\n| Pain-point list (`pain_list`) | 20 | 5–8 with priority and can/can't answer = 20; 2–3 = 10; none = 0 |\n| Pain coverage (`pain_mapping`) | 30 | Only answerable pain points count. ≥80% answered with clear location = 30; 50–79% = 20; <50% = 10; none = 0 |\n| Omissions (`selling_omission`) | 15 | Things the product has but text omits: 0 = 15; 1–2 = 10; ≥3 = 5; all = 0 |\n| Q&A (`qa_coverage`) | 10 | Common doubts answered = 10; partly = 5; none = 0 |\n| Negative reviews (`complaint_fix`) | 5 | Themes addressed = 5; partly = 3; not = 0 (no negative reviews = 5) |\n\nPain points the product can't answer are excluded and not penalised; list them as \"product limits\". Sources: this listing's negative reviews → competitor negative reviews → Q&A → category knowledge.\n\nOutputs: persona card; pain list; pain-to-selling-point map; omission list with where and how to add each; unanswered-but-answerable list.\n\n---\n\n## P0 list (always shown at the top)\n\nTitle over limit; banned characters, promo words, false claims or competitor brands in the title; non-compliant main image; price/promo/contact in bullets; mixed-category variations; core attributes <50% filled; AI people without XMP; suppressed listing; disease/medical claims; other brands' logos; unsupported certifications; the same attribute with conflicting values; A+ or images showing another variant.\n\n---\n\n## Add-on 1: Keywords (unweighted)\n\n| Item | Max | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Title core keyword | 15 | Present = 15; missing = 0 (P1) |\n| Highlights core keyword | 10 | Present = 10; missing = 0 (P1) |\n| Core keyword coverage | 30 | Top 10 all in title or highlights = 30; −3 each; ≥3 missing = 0 |\n| Popular terms | 20 | ≥70% of 20–30 terms = 20; 50–69% = 15; 30–49% = 8; <30% = 0 |\n| Placement | 10 | Core terms in title/highlights; bullets only −2 each |\n| Off-topic words | 10 | −3 each; ≥3 = 0 |\n| Long-tail | 5 | Missing obvious ones −2 |\n\nSensitive words are never recommended and never counted as missing. Benchmark against top search-result titles; don't invent search volumes (\"volume not confirmed\").\n\nOutputs: title/highlights check table; coverage matrix; off-topic table; missing list; backend search-term suggestion.\n\n## Add-on 2: IP risk (unweighted)\n\nP0 confirmed or highly likely; P1 risk signal; P2 preventive. Check trademarks (other brands in text or images, look-alike names), copyright (images, copied text), patents (flag known risks only), misrepresentation (unproven numbers or certifications, misleading images or names).\n\nOutput: type → detail → level → action.\n\n## Add-on 3: Sensitive claims (unweighted)\n\n| Type | Level |\n|---|---|\n| Disease or medical claim | P0 |\n| Absolute/superlative | P1 |\n| Warranty in title or images, guarantee promises in bullets/A+ | P0 |\n| Vague warranty statements | P1 |\n| Unsupported certifications | P0 |\n| False origin | P0 |\n| Quoting Amazon data (bestseller, Prime) | P0 |\n| India: \"pure\" on blends, organic without NPOP, \"NABL certified\", unsupported \"rich in\" | P1 |\n\nOutput: # → where → text → type → risk → safer wording. See `sensitive_words.md`.\n\n## Add-on 4: Consistency (unweighted)\n\nP0 conflicting values or wrong-variant content; P1 fact in one place only; P2 wording differences. Matrix: item × images / text / attributes / A+. See `consistency_check.md`.\n\n## Add-on 5: Nutrition and lab parameters (food; unweighted)\n\nEnergy math (±10%), parts ≤ whole, unit plausibility, category ranges, serving math, %RDA basis, claims vs table, same values everywhere. Deviation = P1; P0 if it creates a false nutrition/health claim. Guide only; parameters only.\n\n## Add-on 6: A+ (unweighted)\n\n| Item | Max | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Exists | 15 | None = 0 |\n| Module variety | 15 | ≥3 types = 15; 1 type = 5 |\n| Sensitive claims | 25 | −8 each; medical −15 |\n| Comparison values | 20 | −8 per conflict; no table = 20 |\n| Consistency with bullets | 15 | A+-only facts −5; conflicts −10 |\n| Keyword additions | 5 | None = 2 |\n| Language | 5 | −2 per typo |\n| A+ images | 10 | Average ≥80 = 10; −5 per image with risky text or another variant |\n| Carousel order | 5 | No carousel = 5; disordered −3 |\n| Module count | 5 | Premium ≥6 or Basic ≥4 = 5; Premium 3–5 = 3; ≤2 = 1 |\n\nAlso check: which variant the A+ shows, duplicate images, alt text. See `aplus_diagnosis.md`.\n\n## Add-on 7: Rufus / Alexa (AEO) (unweighted, 0–100)\n\n| Item | Max | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Title meaning (what + for whom + benefit) | 15 | Keyword list / no use case −5 to −10 |\n| Amazon's Choice | 10 | Yes = 10; no = 0 |\n| Bullet structure (context → fact → benefit) | 15 | Spec list −5; vague words −5 |\n| Query types (use case, comparison, problem-solution, suitability) | 20 | −5 per missing type |\n| Structured attributes | 15 | −3 per missing important attribute |\n| Q&A | 15 | ≥15 substantive = 15; 10–14 = 10; 5–9 = 5; <5 = 0 |\n| Review language | 5 | Positive themes covered = 5; unaddressed negative themes −3 |\n| A+ comparison / FAQ | 5 | Present = 5; none = 2 |\n\nFor each item give the gap and the fix. See `cosmo_rufus_optimization.md` and `aleo_buyer_qa.md`.\n</reference>\n\n<reference file=\"sensitive_words.md\">\n# Sensitive Claims Check\n\nSensitive words are the opposite of keywords: words that must not appear, or must be worded carefully. A word with search value **and** a sensitive meaning (warranty, disease terms) is **never** recommended as a keyword and is never counted as a \"missing keyword\".\n\nScan every field: title, Item Highlights, bullets, A+ text and images, attributes, and text inside carousel images.\n\n---\n\n## 1. India (amazon.in) — FSSAI and consumer law\n\nBased on FSSAI Advertising & Claims Regulations 2018, Labelling & Display Regulations 2020, Organic Food Regulations 2017, and the Consumer Protection Act 2019. These are risk flags; the seller should confirm with a food-regulatory consultant.\n\n| Claim / word | Risk | Notes and safer wording |\n|---|---|---|\n| Disease or treatment claims: \"cures\", \"treats\", \"prevents\", \"for diabetes\", \"diabetic friendly\", \"lowers blood sugar\", \"reduces cholesterol\", \"heart health\", \"cardiovascular\" | **P0** | Not allowed on general foods. Remove. Use neutral facts (\"no added sugar\", \"keto friendly\"). |\n| Health-benefit claims: \"boosts immunity\", \"improves digestion\", \"glowing skin\", \"detox\", \"protects cells from oxidative stress\" | P1 | Need scientific substantiation / approval. Use \"contains naturally occurring antioxidants\", \"a daily addition to your routine\". |\n| \"Pure\" | P1 | Only for single-ingredient foods with nothing added. Not for blends (e.g. honey + turmeric) or products with preservatives. |\n| \"Natural\" | P1 | Only for single foods from a natural source with nothing added; composites may say \"made from natural ingredients\". |\n| \"Fresh\", \"original\", \"authentic\", \"traditional\" | P2 | Need support or remove as filler. |\n| \"Rich in X\" / \"High in X\" / \"Source of X\" | P1 | Must meet thresholds (source ≈ 15% RDA per 100 g or 7.5% per 100 ml; rich/high ≈ twice that). Check against the nutrition table. |\n| \"Zero sugar\", \"sugar free\", \"zero calorie\", \"low calorie\", \"low fat\" | P1 | Must match the nutrition table (zero calorie ≈ ≤4 kcal/100 g or ml). |\n| \"Low glycemic index\" | P1 | Needs GI test data. |\n| \"Organic\" | P1 | India requires NPOP or PGS-India certification and the Jaivik Bharat logo. USDA alone isn't enough for domestic sale; lead with India Organic. |\n| \"Lab certified\", \"NABL certified\" | P1 | Products aren't certified by NABL; labs are accredited. Use \"tested at an NABL-accredited lab\" only if true. |\n| \"FSSAI approved\" | P1 | FSSAI licenses businesses; it doesn't approve products. Use \"FSSAI Lic. No. …\". |\n| Comparisons that criticise other product types on health grounds | P1 | Remove health rows; keep factual rows (taste, ingredients). |\n| Infant use | P1 | Honey must carry \"not for infants under 1 year\"; repeat it in text. |\n| \"Made in India\" | P1 | Must match the country of origin. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. United States (amazon.com) — FDA / FTC\n\n| Claim / word | Risk |\n|---|---|\n| cure, treat, prevent, heal + disease or illness | **P0** |\n| FDA approved (without approval) | **P0** |\n| medical grade, hospital grade (no certification) | P1 |\n| best, #1, world's best, perfect | P1 |\n| guaranteed (without terms) | P1 |\n| safe, child-safe, non-toxic (no certification) | P1 |\n| patented, patent pending (no patent number) | **P0** |\n| eco-friendly, biodegradable, recyclable (no certification) | P1 |\n| Made in USA (not true) | **P0** |\n| UL, ETL, FCC (no certification) | **P0** |\n| Other brands' names or \"like [brand]\" | P1 |\n| Amazon logo or Prime icon in images | **P0** |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Europe and UK\n\n| Claim / word | Risk | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| CE, GS, TÜV, RoHS, UKCA (no certification) | **P0** | Mandatory marks must be real |\n| Made in Germany / UK (not true) | **P0** | Origin claim |\n| Medical-device or healing claims (no MDR) | **P0** | |\n| Test-institute endorsements without licence | **P0** | |\n| Health claims on food not on the EU register | P1 | EU Regulation 1924/2006 |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Japan (amazon.co.jp)\n\n| Claim type | Risk | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pain relief or health effect claims (e.g. \"protects your back from pain\") | **P0** | Describe the function, not the effect: \"supports a natural posture\". |\n| Superlatives (\"strongest\", \"No. 1\", \"best in Japan\") | **P0** | Unprovable |\n| Absolutes (\"perfect\", \"100%\", \"always\") | P1 | |\n| Patent claims without a patent number | **P0** | |\n| PSE / JIS / SG marks without certification | **P0** | |\n| \"Made in Japan\" (not true) | P1 | |\n| Quoting Amazon bestseller status | **P0** | |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Warranty statements (all marketplaces)\n\nAmazon policy:\n1. **Title:** never mention a warranty (P0).\n2. **Images:** no warranty or guarantee badges or text (P0).\n3. **Bullets / A+:** no guarantee promises such as \"full refund\", \"unconditional guarantee\", \"lifetime guarantee\", \"100% guarantee\" (P0).\n4. **Seller's own warranty** can't be promoted on the detail page (P1).\n5. A manufacturer warranty can be mentioned only with terms, duration, region and provider name and address; otherwise don't mention it.\n6. **Where to put a real warranty:** US — product edit page → Safety & Compliance → Warranty Description. India — Growth → Browse programs → Add Warranty info (Excel upload; shown as a separate warranty card).\n\nWarranty is never a required bullet; don't penalise its absence. Build trust with verifiable facts instead: what's in the box, dimensions, materials, test data, \"returns per Amazon's return policy\".\n\n---\n\n## 6. Process\n\n1. Scan all fields, including A+ and image text (A+ is checked separately; compliant bullets don't make the A+ compliant).\n2. Classify each hit and give a risk level.\n3. Give safer wording for every hit.\n4. Remove sensitive words from keyword suggestions; note \"excluded: sensitive\".\n\n## 7. Output template\n\n| # | Where | Text | Type | Risk | Suggested wording |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Image 6 | Diabetic Friendly | Disease claim | P0 | Keto Friendly |\n</reference>\n\n<reference file=\"multi_site_adaptation.md\">\n# Marketplace Parameters\n\nLoad the target marketplace's parameters before fetching a listing, so the page comes back in the right language, currency and delivery region. After fetching, check language and currency.\n\n## 1. Marketplace matrix\n\n| Marketplace | Domain | `language` | Currency | Symbol | Title limit* | Keepa prefix |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| United States | amazon.com | en_US | USD | $ | **75 (enforced)** | 1 |\n| United Kingdom | amazon.co.uk | en_GB | GBP | £ | 200 (reference) | 2 |\n| Germany | amazon.de | de_DE | EUR | € | 200 (reference) | 3 |\n| France | amazon.fr | fr_FR | EUR | € | 200 (reference) | 4 |\n| Japan | amazon.co.jp | ja_JP | JPY | ¥ | 200 (reference) | 5 |\n| Canada | amazon.ca | en_CA | CAD | C$ | 200 (reference) | 6 |\n| Italy | amazon.it | it_IT | EUR | € | 200 (reference) | 8 |\n| Spain | amazon.es | es_ES | EUR | € | 200 (reference) | 9 |\n| **India** | **amazon.in** | **en_IN** | **INR** | **₹** | **75 (enforced); Item Highlights 75** | 11 |\n| Mexico | amazon.com.mx | es_MX | MXN | MX$ | 200 (reference) | 12 |\n| Australia | amazon.com.au | en_AU | AUD | A$ | 200 (reference) | 13 |\n| Brazil | amazon.com.br | pt_BR | BRL | R$ | 200 (reference) | 15 |\n| Singapore | amazon.sg | en_SG | SGD | S$ | 200 (reference) | 16 |\n| Netherlands | amazon.nl | nl_NL | EUR | € | 200 (reference) | 17 |\n| UAE | amazon.ae | en_AE | AED | AED | 200 (reference) | 19 |\n| Sweden | amazon.se | sv_SE | SEK | kr | 200 (reference) | 21 |\n| Poland | amazon.pl | pl_PL | PLN | zł | 200 (reference) | 22 |\n| Turkey | amazon.com.tr | tr_TR | TRY | ₺ | 200 (reference) | 24 |\n| Saudi Arabia | amazon.sa | en_AE / ar_SA | SAR | SAR | 200 (reference) | 25 |\n| Egypt | amazon.eg | ar_EG | EGP | EGP | 200 (reference) | 26 |\n\n\\* US: 75-character title rule enforced. India: title and Item Highlights are both limited to 75 characters (confirmed by the seller in Seller Central, Nov 2026). Elsewhere, follow what Seller Central shows; 200 is a reference only. Always count characters with a script before giving a title or highlights.\n\nKeepa price history: `https://keepa.com/#!product/{prefix}-{ASIN}`.\n\n## 2. Fetch parameters\n\n- Product page: `https://www.amazon.in/dp/{ASIN}?language=en_IN&currency=INR` (swap domain, language and currency).\n- Optional delivery location: `&postalCode=` (US 10001, UK SW1A 1AA, DE 10115, IN 400001).\n- Cookies (more reliable): `i18n-prefs={CURRENCY}; lc-main={language}`.\n- `Accept-Language` header: en-US, en-GB, en-IN, de-DE, fr-FR, it-IT, es-ES, en-CA, en-AU, ja-JP, pt-BR, en-AE etc., matching the marketplace.\n- Reviews: `/product-reviews/{ASIN}?language=...`\n- Variant detail: `/dp/{ASIN}?th=1&psc=1&language=...&currency=...`\n- Search (keyword research): `/s?k={keyword}&language=...`\n\nPrefer the built-in browser over plain HTTP fetches: Item Highlights (`.dp-title-differentiators`) and some modules only render in a browser.\n\n## 3. After-fetch checks\n\n1. **Language:** page text matches the marketplace. If the interface is local but the seller's bullets are in another language, that is a localisation gap in the listing, not a fetch error.\n2. **Currency:** price symbol matches (₹ for India, $ for US, £ UK, € EU).\n3. **Region:** BSR and delivery text match the marketplace.\n\n## 4. Language expectations by marketplace\n\n| Marketplace | Listing language | Common problems |\n|---|---|---|\n| US | English, US spelling (color, center) | UK spelling mixed in |\n| UK / India / Australia / Singapore | English (UK/Indian spelling acceptable) | Inconsistent spelling within one listing |\n| Germany / France / Italy / Spain / Netherlands | Local language | English left in bullets |\n| Japan | Japanese | English-only bullets, literal translation |\n| UAE / Saudi | English and/or Arabic | No Arabic at all on Arabic-first categories |\n\n## 5. Marketplace-specific compliance notes\n\n| Rule | US | India | EU | UK | Japan |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Title limit | 75 enforced | 75 enforced (+ highlights 75) | Check Seller Central | Check Seller Central | Check Seller Central |\n| Food regulator | FDA | FSSAI | EFSA / national | FSA | MHLW / CAA |\n| Organic marks | USDA Organic | NPOP / PGS-India + Jaivik Bharat logo | EU organic leaf | UK organic | JAS |\n| Electrical certification | UL / ETL / FCC | BIS | CE | UKCA / CE | PSE |\n| Units | inch / lb | cm / kg / ml | cm / kg | cm / kg | cm / kg |\n\nWhen a certification isn't visible on the page, say \"this category usually needs X in this marketplace and the page doesn't show it\" rather than calling it a violation; it may be in backend attributes or A+.\n</reference>\n\n<reference file=\"amazon_listing_rules_2025.md\">\n# Amazon Listing Rules (2026 summary)\n\nBased on Amazon policy pages and updates up to mid-2026. Use as a reference; Seller Central is the final authority.\n\n## 1. Title\n\n- **75 characters including spaces** (from 27 July 2026, all categories except media). Not applied in Egypt, Saudi Arabia, Turkey, UAE. **India: 75, confirmed by the seller.** Over-limit titles get replaced with AI-suggested versions.\n- Title + Item Highlights together describe the product; move detail from the title into highlights.\n- Banned characters: `! $ ? _ { } ^ ¬ ¦`. No promo/subjective words (\"best seller\", \"cheap\", \"free shipping\", \"best quality\"), no keyword stuffing, no uncertified claims.\n- Structure: brand + product name + 1–2 core keywords + key attribute (size, pack). Put colour/size in the variation theme, not the title.\n- Amazon's AI tool: Manage All Inventory → Edit → View enhancements (brand owners have 14 days to review).\n\nExample: \"Premium Stainless Steel Insulated Water Bottle – BPA-Free\" (62) + highlights \"Keeps drinks cold 24 hours, hot 12 hours. Ideal for gym, office, and outdoor adventures.\"\n\n## 2. Item Highlights (new, July 2026)\n\n- US: 125 characters. **India: 75 characters (confirmed by the seller).**\n- Indexed for search; shown under the title (separate grey line on mobile; joined with \"|\" on desktop and in the page `<title>`).\n- Content: materials, use cases, compatibility, size range, proof points. Comma-separated short attributes.\n- Not a second title, keyword dump or repeat of bullets.\n\n## 3. Bullets\n\n- Up to 5; about 100–250 characters each (some tools suggest ≥150 for quality scores).\n- Start with a capital; no emoji; no all-caps except short headers/acronyms; no repetition.\n- Banned: price, promotion, shipping, seller information.\n- One feature + benefit per bullet.\n- Common order (industry practice, **not an Amazon rule; don't deduct for it**): hero benefit → use case → differentiator → specs/compatibility → trust facts.\n\n## 4. Backend search terms\n\n- 249 **bytes**; going over by one byte can void the whole field.\n- Don't repeat words in the title/highlights/bullets; no commas; no brand names (yours or others'); no ASINs.\n- Use synonyms, alternative and phonetic spellings, local-language terms (e.g. Hindi transliterations on amazon.in), long-tail terms.\n\n## 5. Images\n\n**Main:** pure white (RGB 255,255,255); ≥1000 px (2000+ recommended); product ≥85% of the frame; no added text, watermark, logo, border or props not included. Real product.\n\n**Secondary:** up to 9; at least 6 images including one lifestyle image recommended; use all 7 slots plus a video. Types: lifestyle, size reference, detail close-up, infographic, package contents.\n\nSize-reference images are often missed: look for measurement lines, reference objects (hand, coin, phone) or side-by-side size comparisons.\n\n**AI-generated realistic people (from 23 July 2026):** add `contains-synthetic-performer` to the XMP `dc:subject` field before upload. Not needed for real photos (even if retouched), cartoon characters or images without people.\n\n**Video:** commonly lifts conversion 10–20%.\n\n## 6. A+ content\n\n- Indexed for search; needs Brand Registry.\n- Low-quality A+ may be collapsed behind \"See more\" (2026 quality analysis).\n- Images ≥970×600 (standard modules); text ≤30% of an image; JPG/PNG/BMP, RGB, <2 MB, ≥72 dpi; no GIF.\n- **Alt text must describe the image accurately** (no keyword stuffing).\n- Banned: promotions, review quotes, comparisons with other brands (own products allowed), warranty/returns information, contact details, QR codes, shipping. Certification claims must name the body and year. Avoid \"eco-friendly\", \"biodegradable\", \"compostable\".\n- Mobile first (>70% of shoppers).\n- Use Brand Story (free) and a comparison table of 3–5 own products.\n\n## 7. Listing Quality Score (from around May 2026, inferred)\n\nReported factors: title completeness, image count and resolution, bullet depth, A+/description, backend term use, rating and review velocity (below ~3.8★ flagged), price competitiveness. ASINs scoring under ~65 reportedly lose non-branded organic visibility (15–40% in some categories).\n\n## 8. Other 2026 changes\n\n- Public \"frequently returned\" label for high-return products (US, CA, EU; Feb 2026).\n- Variations with functional differences can no longer share reviews (Feb 2026; transition to 31 May 2026).\n- Payment reserve: funds released 7 days after delivery (Mar 2026).\n- Commingled inventory ended (Mar 2026): brand sellers with GTINs don't need Amazon barcodes; others must label.\n\n## 9. Suppression / quality risks\n\nTitle over limit; banned characters; empty Item Highlights; non-compliant main image; fewer than 6 images or no lifestyle image; price/promo/shipping in bullets; very short bullets; competitor brands or ASINs in search terms; AI people without XMP; collapsed A+; wrong category; missing key attributes; rating under ~3.8★; high-return label.\n</reference>\n\n<reference file=\"cosmo_rufus_optimization.md\">\n# COSMO, Rufus and Alexa — How AI Shopping Reads a Listing\n\nReference for Step 9 (AEO). Figures and mechanics are from Amazon publications and industry reports up to mid-2026.\n\n## 1. The three layers\n\n```\nShopper question → Rufus works out intent → COSMO walks the knowledge graph → A10 keyword match + conversion signals → ranked results\n```\nOptimising only one layer leaves the others short of data.\n\n- **COSMO:** Amazon's LLM-built commonsense knowledge graph (SIGMOD 2024 paper). It understands intent, not just keywords.\n- **Rufus:** the shopper-facing assistant. It reads the title, Item Highlights, bullets, A+ (text and image text via OCR), reviews, Q&A and images, then answers and recommends. Reported: Rufus-recommended products get 3–5× the CTR of standard organic results.\n- **Alexa for Shopping:** voice; relies heavily on Amazon's Choice, ratings (≥4★), and bullets that each answer one question.\n\nRufus doesn't recommend products it isn't confident about. Complete, consistent information raises confidence; vague, missing or contradictory information lowers it.\n\n## 2. Semantic relations COSMO looks for\n\n| Relation | Question | Where in the listing | Common gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| isA | What is it? | Title | Brand + adjectives, no clear product type |\n| usedFor | What is it for? | Highlights, bullets (\"for X, when Y\") | Attributes listed, no use case |\n| capableOf | What can it do? | Bullets with numbers | \"Durable\" with no figure |\n| cause | What result does it give? | Bullets, A+ | Feature without outcome (keep within claim rules) |\n| userGroup | Who is it for? | Highlights, bullets | \"Perfect for everyone\" |\n\n## 3. What Rufus reads (2026)\n\n1. Title (75 characters) — identity and core keyword\n2. Item Highlights — comparison info, use case; read early, shown under the title\n3. Bullets\n4. A+ text and image text\n5. Reviews (sentiment and recurring phrases)\n6. Q&A\n7. Images (OCR, scene recognition)\n\n**\"Why this product\" sentence:** keep one consistent value statement across the title, highlights, bullet 1 and A+ module 1; Rufus often quotes it.\n\n## 4. Five Rufus factors\n\n1. **Context:** scenario language (\"lightweight rain jacket for monsoon hikes\", not just \"waterproof jacket\").\n2. **Behaviour signals:** seasonal and event relevance (festivals, back-to-school, winter).\n3. **Structured attributes:** most sellers leave 30+ fields empty; filling them is the biggest opportunity.\n4. **Performance:** CTR, conversion, reviews, returns; a \"frequently returned\" badge hurts recommendations.\n5. **Cross-surface consistency:** title, highlights, bullets, A+ and Store must agree.\n\n## 5. Strategies\n\n1. **Title + highlights together:** title = brand + type + 1–2 keywords + fit info; highlights = materials · use · compatibility · size, or proof points. Move detail out of the title.\n2. **Intent over keywords:** connect each feature to a benefit and a situation, e.g. \"moisture-wicking fabric to keep you dry during intense workouts\".\n3. **Bullets as answers:** each bullet answers one buyer question (durability, who it's for, how it differs, size/compatibility, what's in the box). Industry bullet order (hero → use case → differentiator → specs → trust) is a guide, **not a scoring rule**.\n4. **Answer likely questions** in text and an A+ FAQ.\n5. **Images:** show real use; put key facts in large, OCR-readable text; tag AI-generated people with XMP.\n6. **Fill everything:** attributes, highlights, search terms (synonyms, local and phonetic spellings), alt text, A+.\n7. **Use Amazon's AI suggestions** (Manage All Inventory → Edit → View enhancements) but review them.\n\n## 6. Intent-coverage check (community framework, not an official COSMO score)\n\n| Intent | Points | Check |\n|---|---|---|\n| usedFor | 25 | Specific use cases, not just attributes |\n| cause | 20 | Outcome described (within claim rules) |\n| userGroup | 20 | 1–2 clear buyer groups |\n| capableOf | 15 | Numbers, not adjectives |\n| isA | 10 | Clear product type |\n| Attribute → intent bridge | 10 | Each key attribute explained in buyer terms |\n\n**Query types to cover:** scenario (\"best X for Y\"), problem (\"X that doesn't Y\"), persona (\"X for kids\"), comparison (\"X vs Y\").\n\n**Common problems:**\n- *Spec-sheet syndrome:* only specs. Fix: add a use/benefit to each.\n- *Adjective dependence:* \"premium\", \"high quality\". Fix: replace with verifiable numbers.\n- *No audience:* \"for everyone\". Fix: name 1–2 groups.\n- *No scenario:* features without use cases. Fix: \"for X, when Y\".\n\n## 7. Quick AEO checklist\n\n1. Title ≤ limit and states type + key value.\n2. Item Highlights filled with use, proof or compatibility.\n3. Same core value in title, highlights, bullet 1 and A+ module 1.\n4. Use cases in bullets.\n5. Structured attributes complete (target ≥95% for Alexa-style queries).\n6. Key facts readable in image text.\n7. A+ not collapsed; has FAQ or comparison.\n8. AI people tagged (if any).\n9. No high-return badge.\n10. Bullets can each stand alone as an answer.\n11. Q&A: ≥10–15 substantive answers, including edge cases.\n12. Review wording matches the main selling points.\n13. Rating ≥4.0★ (Alexa and Rufus rarely recommend below).\n14. Covers isA, usedFor, capableOf, cause and userGroup.\n15. Not a spec sheet; not adjective-led.\n16. Covers scenario, problem, persona and comparison queries.\n17. Consistent data across surfaces (see `cdq_data_quality.md`).\n</reference>\n\n<reference file=\"aleo_buyer_qa.md\">\n# Answer-Engine Optimisation (AEO) and Buyer Questions\n\nAEO = making the listing the source an AI shopping assistant quotes when a shopper asks a question. The question changes from \"do I rank for keyword X?\" to \"when someone asks Y, does Alexa/Rufus cite my listing?\"\n\n## 1. COSMO vs AEO\n\n| | COSMO intent coverage | AEO |\n|---|---|---|\n| Role | Gatekeeper: is the listing a candidate? | Answer engine: is it quoted? |\n| Checks | Is the intent written down? | Can it answer the actual question? |\n| Focus | Intent semantics, attributes, persona | Quotable bullets, Q&A, review wording, rating threshold |\n\n## 2. What Alexa / Rufus reads (by priority)\n\n1. Bullets — quoted as standalone answers\n2. Customer Q&A — a major index field since 2026; source of edge-case wording\n3. Reviews — treated as \"ground truth\", more trusted than marketing copy\n4. Title — read as a sentence\n5. A+ text (comparison and scenario modules)\n6. Brand Store content\n7. All structured attributes, including optional ones\n8. Image alt text and OCR'd image text\n\n**Hard thresholds (reported):**\n- Rating below 4.0★: excluded from many recommendations (an entry condition, not a ranking factor).\n- Attribute completeness below ~90–95%: invisible to whole classes of conversational queries.\n\nIf reviews repeatedly say \"loud\" or \"leaks\", the listing won't be chosen for \"quiet\" or \"leak-proof\" questions, whatever the bullets claim.\n\n## 3. Three question types\n\n| Type | Example | Listing needs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scenario / use case | \"What's a good honey for my morning tea?\" | Use cases and compatibility in title and bullets |\n| Comparison / filter | \"Which sweetener has no erythritol under ₹800?\" | Comparable numbers and facts in bullets |\n| Persona / problem | \"Juice for someone avoiding sugar\" | Target buyer and the problem it solves (within claim rules) |\n\n## 4. Six levers\n\n1. **Bullets as standalone answers:** one bullet = one question = one complete, verifiable answer with a number or use case. Drop \"premium\", \"durable\", \"high quality\". Cover: will it last, is it right for my use, does it work with X, how easy is it, what's in the box.\n   - Before: \"High-quality materials for maximum durability.\"\n   - After: \"58 dB noise level, quieter than normal conversation, with a HEPA H13 filter that captures 99.97% of pollen, for rooms up to 800 sq ft.\"\n2. **Title as use-case attributes**, not a keyword list:\n   - Weak: \"Running Shoes Men Black Blue Waterproof 42 43 44\"\n   - Strong: \"Men's Running Shoes with Arch Support for Flat Feet, Size 10\"\n3. **Q&A as an index field:** find the 10 most common questions (competitor Q&A, reviews, Rufus prompts); answer them in detail; add an A+ FAQ; recheck after updates. Never post fake questions or answers.\n4. **Reviews as a lever:** recurring phrases in reviews help ranking for matching queries. Encourage honest reviews through Amazon-approved channels only (Request a Review, Vine). Never ask for specific wording in exchange for anything, and never use friends, family or staff.\n5. **Images that show intent:** real use settings, scale references (hand, cup), specific numbers instead of generic icons, a scene image per variant.\n6. **Rating threshold:** below 4.0★, fix product quality and negative-review themes before other AEO work.\n\n## 5. AEO checklist\n\n**Quotability:** each bullet answers one question; has a number or specific use; title has extractable use-case attributes; the 5 core questions are answered.\n\n**Q&A:** ≥10 answered questions; edge cases covered; answers specific.\n\n**Reviews:** wording matches main selling points; no cluster of negatives contradicting claims; rating ≥4.0★.\n\n**Attributes:** ≥95% filled; intended use, audience, material/ingredients, certifications, compatibility filled.\n\n**Images:** real-use scene images; specific numbers in infographics; descriptive alt text.\n\n## 6. AEO vs traditional SEO\n\n| | Traditional SEO | AEO |\n|---|---|---|\n| Goal | Keyword rank | Being quoted as the answer |\n| Method | Keyword coverage | Attribute completeness, fact density |\n| Main sources | Title, bullets, search terms | Reviews, Q&A, A+, attributes |\n| Title | Keyword list | Quotable use-case attributes |\n| Bullets | Feature list | Question–answer pairs |\n| Q&A | Optional | Major index field |\n| Reviews | Trust signal | Active lever |\n| Images | Looks | Visual intent |\n\n## 7. Timeline (reported)\n\n- Jun 2024: COSMO paper at SIGMOD 2024.\n- Late 2024: Rufus launched.\n- 2025: Rufus monthly users +140%; recommended products 3–5× CTR.\n- Nov 2025: Alexa for Shopping adds price-triggered buying and auto-replenishment.\n- Jan 2026: Alexa shows 30- and 90-day price trends.\n- 13 May 2026: Rufus renamed Alexa for Shopping in some marketplaces; voice and cross-device context added.\n</reference>";
